The first mass has kinetic energy $T_1=\frac12mr^2\dot\theta^2.$ The second mass moves both because the first mass moves and because the second rod rotates relative to the first mass. Its velocity components are $\begin{aligned}
 v_{2x}
 &=r\cos\theta\,\dot\theta+r\cos\phi\,\dot\phi,\\
 v_{2y}
 &=-r\sin\theta\,\dot\theta-r\sin\phi\,\dot\phi.
 \end{aligned}$

